# Source formatting — vertical alignment

When editing or adding code in this repo, **vertically align** punctuation within a visual block where it is practical and does not fight an auto-formatter.

## Align these (when possible)

- **Dict literals** — pad keys so `:` line up in the same dict (see `MAIN_WIDGETS` in `tests/integration/gui/specs.py`).
- **Dataclass / state fields** — pad field names so `:` align; on rows with unions, pad type names so each `|` (`| None`, `| NoChange`, …) stays in a fixed column with single spaces around it (see `TextChange` in `ConnectEd/widgets/graphics/items/text.py`, `ItemBefore` in `ConnectEd/widgets/graphics/scenes/drawing/cmd/edit/appearance.py`).
- **Grouped assignments** — consecutive related `name : value` or `name = value` lines in specs, constants, or small tables.
- **Multi-line calls** — keyword arguments, especially in validators and scripting; align `=` or `:` if the project uses typed kwargs.
- **Multi-line signatures** — pad parameter names so `:` align on wrapped `def` lines (see `withModal` in `ConnectEd/scripting/qt/modal.py`). When some parameters use union types, find the row whose type name is longest before the first `|` (no wriggle room); pad shorter type names to that width so every `|` in the block shares the same column — do not pad after `None` or other union members instead.

## When extending an aligned block

If a new name is longer than existing rows, **re-align the whole block** to the new longest name. Do not leave a mix of old and new column widths — that breaks vertical alignment of `:`, `=`, and `|`.

When a block mixes `T | NoChange` and `T | None | NoChange`, pad type names so `| None` and `| NoChange` each stay in fixed columns; use single spaces around every `|` (see `ItemBefore` in `ConnectEd/widgets/graphics/scenes/drawing/cmd/edit/appearance.py`). The longest type name before the first `|` sets the column — pad shorter names, not trailing union members.

## Do not force it when

- A block is huge or deeply nested (e.g. large menu trees) — alignment adds noise without clarity.
- A formatter (Ruff, Black) would immediately undo the change — prefer project formatter settings if we adopt one for alignment later.
- It would require very wide spacing or harm diff readability.

## Example (dict keys → types)

```python
MAIN_WIDGETS : dict[str, type] = {
    "menuBar"    : MenuBar,
    "statusBar"  : StatusBar,
    "central"    : MdiArea,
    "Navigator"  : NavigatorDock,
    "Messages"   : MessagesViewDock,
    "Transcript" : TranscriptViewDock,
    "Log"        : LogViewDock,
}
```

Keep existing project spacing around `:` in annotations (e.g. `def foo(x : int) -> None`).

## Example (multi-line `def`)

```python
def withModal(
    self    : Self,
    opener  : Callable[[], None],
    onModal : Callable[[], None],
) -> None:
    ...
```

## Example (multi-line signature — union `|` anchored to longest type)

`parent` fixes the column; shorter types pad before `|`, not after `None`:

```python
def __init__(
    self       : Self,
    pos        : QPointF       | None = None,
    color      : QColor        | None = None,
    font       : str           | None = None,
    parent     : QGraphicsItem | None = None,
) -> None:
    ...
```

## Example (dataclass fields)

```python
@dataclass
class TextState:
    align_h    : AlignH
    align_v    : AlignV
    width      : float
    pad_bottom : float   # longest name sets column width for whole block
    pad_left   : float
    color      : QColor | None
    font       : str    | None
```

## Example (union types with `| NoChange`)

When some rows omit `| None`, pad bare types to the same width so `| NoChange` still lines up:

```python
@dataclass
class TextChange:
    width      : float          | NoChange = NO_CHANGE
    pad_bottom : float          | NoChange = NO_CHANGE
    color      : QColor | None | NoChange = NO_CHANGE
    font       : str    | None | NoChange = NO_CHANGE
```

## Snake_case locals for ConnectEd types

Use `connected_` in snake_case and `connected` in camelCase for ConnectEd type locals (not `connect_ed_` / `connectEd`), e.g. `connected_bar`, `connected_menu`. GUI driver uses `driver.window()` → `Window`. Third-party names (`MenuBar`, `QMenuBar`) stay as defined.
